<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Crypteia</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Krypteia, Krupteia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Crypteia was an institution of the ancient Spartan state, made up of young upper-class men, probably aged between 21 and 30, who had passed through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agoge">agoge</a> with distinction. Ancient accounts differ on its purpose: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> and Heraclides Lembus, possibly following a lost work by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a>, describe kryptai sent into the countryside with daggers to kill <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helots">helots</a>, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephor">ephors</a> declared war on the helots each autumn, while others saw it as military training or an initiation ordeal. Aristotle credited it to the lawgiver Lycurgus, but Plutarch thought it was introduced, if at all, after the helot revolt of the mid-460s BCE. Modern historians often translate the name as secret police.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name comes from the Greek kruptos, meaning hidden or secret, and its members were called kryptai. Its structure is debated, partly because few classical texts mention it and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xenophon">Xenophon</a>'s Constitution of the Lacedaemonians does not. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> it was established by the Spartan lawgiver Lycurgus, and there is no known date for its founding. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a>, writing centuries later, doubted that Lycurgus would have put such harsh customs into the Spartan constitution and thought the Crypteia had been introduced, if at all, only after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helots">helot</a> revolt that followed an earthquake in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> in the mid-460s BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Its ranks were young upper-class Spartans, probably aged between 21 and 30, known as hêbôntes, who had finished the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agoge">agoge</a> so well that officials marked them as future leaders. They may have been chosen as those judged most intelligent. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a>, they went barefoot in winter, slept without cover, had no attendants, and roamed the countryside by night and by day. The length of service is unknown, and a scholion on Plato's Laws suggests it may have been a single year.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> and Heraclides Lembus, who may both draw on a lost work of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a>, describe kryptai sent into the countryside armed only with daggers, hiding by day and killing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helots">helots</a> on the roads by night, sometimes working in the fields by day. Aristotle added that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephor">ephors</a> declared war on the helots each autumn so that they could be killed without breach of religion. Some scholars see a form of secret police, others military training like the Athenian ephebia, and the French historian Henri Jeanmaire saw a rite of passage. Plutarch also names Damoteles as commander of the Crypteia at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sellasia">Battle of Sellasia</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleomenes-iii">Cleomenes III</a> sent him to observe the army's rear and flanks, which shows it as a unit with a commander.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Some have linked the Crypteia to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thucydides">Thucydides</a>' account of how the Spartans, in the eighth year of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnesian-war">Peloponnesian War</a>, invited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helots">helots</a> to claim freedom for bravery and then did away with about two thousand of them, though Thucydides does not name it. Whether the Crypteia was disbanded after Sellasia, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleomenes-iii">Cleomenes III</a> began emancipating helots, is only speculation. The historian Stephen Hodkinson notes that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> portray it in almost contradictory ways, and that modern scholars have adopted a composite understanding of it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Crypteia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Each year the ephors formally declared war on the helots, and the <b>krypteia</b>, a body of young men, could kill helots without penalty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sparta">Sparta · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Each year the ephors declared war on the helots, so that killing one brought no religious pollution, and in the <b>Krypteia</b>, according to Plutarch, young Spartiates were sent into the countryside to kill helots.</blockquote><a href="/societies/sparta">Social Hierarchy of Ancient Sparta</a></li></ul></section>
